Subject: plug the wasi-libc gaps in QtCore's generic UNIX backends

WHAT / WHY -- one hunk per missing wasi-libc facility. Each names the symbol
or header that is absent and what breaks without the guard.

  src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix_p.h
      <sys/wait.h> does not exist in the wasi sysroot (wasip2 has no
      fork/exec, so nothing can be waited on). This header is included by
      nearly every UNIX TU in QtCore, so the missing file stops the build on
      file one. Its only user, qt_safe_waitpid(), lives under
      QT_CONFIG(processenvironment), which this port has OFF.

  src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix.cpp
      qt_ignore_sigpipe() uses `struct sigaction` and ::sigaction unguarded.
      wasi-libc has NEITHER even with -D_WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL -- the struct is
      an incomplete type and the function is undeclared;
      libwasi-emulated-signal.a defines only signal, raise, psignal,
      strsignal, bsd_signal and __sysv_signal. Made a no-op: there is no
      process to send SIGPIPE to.

  src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_unix.cpp
      The nastiest of the four, because it compiles and fails at LINK. The
      wasi sysroot SHIPS <sys/eventfd.h>, declaring eventfd/eventfd_read/
      eventfd_write and defining EFD_CLOEXEC, but NO wasi-sdk library defines
      any of the three (verified with llvm-nm over libc.a, librt.a,
      libpthread.a and every libwasi-emulated-*). So `__has_include` says yes,
      UsingEventfd becomes true, and the link dies on three undefined symbols.
      Excluding WASI from the __has_include test also leaves EFD_CLOEXEC
      undefined, which is what removes the three `#ifdef EFD_CLOEXEC` blocks
      further down; QThreadPipe then falls back to qt_safe_pipe().

      ...and a second, independent hunk in the same file makes that fallback
      failing NON-FATAL. pipe() is present in wasi-libc but is routed through
      its descriptor-table vtable, and a wasip2 host that does not implement it
      returns ENOTSUP -- stock `wasmtime run` does exactly this. Upstream then
      qFatal()s in QEventDispatcherUNIXPrivate's constructor, so QApplication
      cannot be constructed AT ALL and the whole of QtWidgets is unreachable.
      VERIFIED: before this hunk the smoke app died with "QThreadPipe: Unable
      to create pipe: Not supported" / "Cannot continue without a thread pipe";
      after it, the same binary runs to completion.

      The thread pipe exists solely so another thread or a signal handler can
      wake a blocked event loop. This port is QT_FEATURE_thread=OFF on a
      runtime with neither threads nor signals, so nothing can call wakeUp()
      from outside the thread already inside processEvents(). fds stays
      {-1,-1}: prepare() hands poll() a negative fd, which POSIX requires it to
      ignore, and wakeUp()/check() keep the wakeUps flag working so a wakeup
      posted before processEvents() is still observed.

  src/corelib/kernel/qcoreapplication.cpp
      The setuid sanity check calls geteuid() and getuid(). wasi-libc declares
      NEITHER -- unistd.h keeps the whole block behind
      __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream -- so this is a compile error, not a link
      error. Nothing to check either: a component has no uid, cannot be setuid,
      and its authority comes from the capabilities the host handed it.

  src/corelib/io/qfilesystemengine_unix.cpp
      Three things. (a) <grp.h> and <pwd.h> do not exist; guarded. (b) The two
      existing `!defined(Q_OS_WASM)` guards around getpwuid/getgrgid gain
      Q_OS_WASI -- there is no user or group database. (c) moveFileToTrash
      takes the same ENOSYS stub Android takes, because the freedesktop trash
      implementation calls ::getuid() twice, to name $topdir/.Trash-$uid and to
      verify directory ownership.

  src/corelib/io/qstandardpaths_unix.cpp
      Two geteuid() call sites in the XDG_RUNTIME_DIR logic. Rather than
      restructure both blocks, a file-local `geteuid()` returning 0 is defined
      under Q_OS_WASI. Returning 0 is not a placeholder: wasi's stat fills
      st_uid with 0, so the ownership check in checkXdgRuntimeDir() compares
      equal against a directory this component just created. Returning
      anything else would make QStandardPaths::RuntimeLocation always come
      back empty.

UPSTREAM
  yes for the six guard hunks -- they are the same shape as the Q_OS_WASM,
  Q_OS_INTEGRITY and Q_OS_VXWORKS guards already sitting next to them, and
  three of them (sys/wait.h, sigaction, eventfd) are arguably about any
  POSIX-incomplete libc, not about wasi specifically.

  not-as-is for the thread-pipe hunk: upstream's real answer is a dedicated
  event dispatcher rather than a POSIX one limping without its wakeup channel,
  and that is what the wk QPA will bring (a QAbstractEventDispatcherV2 blocking
  in wkgfx_wait_frame()). This hunk is what makes Widgets testable in the
  meantime, and it should be revisited then.

  not-as-is for the qstandardpaths_unix.cpp geteuid shim. A file-local
  redefinition of a POSIX function is not something upstream would take; the
  upstream shape is a QT_CONFIG feature or a q_geteuid() in qcore_unix_p.h.
  Carried as-is here because it is two lines and touches one file.

---
diff --git a/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix_p.h b/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix_p.h
index bf955db..7b1a6e2 100644
--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix_p.h
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix_p.h
@@ -40,7 +40,14 @@
 #endif
 
 #include <chrono>
-#include <sys/wait.h>
+#ifndef Q_OS_WASI
+// wasi-libc has no <sys/wait.h> at all: wasip2 has no fork/exec, so nothing
+// can be waited on. This header is included by nearly every UNIX TU in QtCore,
+// so the missing file would otherwise stop the build on file one.
+// The only user, qt_safe_waitpid(), is itself already inside
+// QT_CONFIG(processenvironment) territory, which is OFF for this port.
+#  include <sys/wait.h>
+#endif
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
 
diff --git a/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix.cpp b/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix.cpp
index 6861251..2dbe671 100644
--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qcore_unix.cpp
@@ -22,6 +22,13 @@ QT_BEGIN_NAMESPACE
 
 void qt_ignore_sigpipe() noexcept // noexcept: sigaction(2) is not a Posix Cancellation Point
 {
+#ifdef Q_OS_WASI
+    // wasi-libc has neither `struct sigaction` (incomplete type) nor
+    // ::sigaction, even with -D_WASI_EMULATED_SIGNAL --
+    // libwasi-emulated-signal.a defines only signal/raise/psignal/strsignal.
+    // There is nothing to ignore either: wasip2 has no process to send SIGPIPE
+    // to, and a write to a closed stream returns an error instead.
+#else
     // Set to ignore SIGPIPE once only.
     Q_CONSTINIT static QBasicAtomicInt atom = Q_BASIC_ATOMIC_INITIALIZER(0);
     if (!atom.loadRelaxed()) {
@@ -33,6 +40,7 @@ void qt_ignore_sigpipe() noexcept // noexcept: sigaction(2) is not a Posix Cance
         ::sigaction(SIGPIPE, &noaction, nullptr);
         atom.storeRelaxed(1);
     }
+#endif
 }
 
 QByteArray qt_readlink(const char *path)
diff --git a/src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_unix.cpp b/src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_unix.cpp
index fe54750..5894b22 100644
--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_unix.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qeventdispatcher_unix.cpp
@@ -20,7 +20,14 @@
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 
-#if __has_include(<sys/eventfd.h>)
+// NOT just __has_include on wasi: the wasi sysroot SHIPS <sys/eventfd.h>,
+// declaring eventfd/eventfd_read/eventfd_write and defining EFD_CLOEXEC, but
+// NO wasi-sdk library defines any of the three (verified with llvm-nm over
+// libc.a, librt.a, libpthread.a and every libwasi-emulated-*). Taking the
+// eventfd path here therefore compiles cleanly and fails at link with three
+// undefined symbols. Forcing UsingEventfd=false makes QThreadPipe fall back to
+// qt_safe_pipe(), and pipe() does exist on wasip2.
+#if __has_include(<sys/eventfd.h>) && !defined(Q_OS_WASI)
 #  include <sys/eventfd.h>
 static constexpr bool UsingEventfd = true;
 #else
@@ -135,8 +142,29 @@ bool QThreadPipe::init()
     if (!UsingEventfd)
         ret = qt_safe_pipe(fds, O_NONBLOCK);
     if (ret == -1) {
+#  ifdef Q_OS_WASI
+        // Not fatal here. The thread pipe exists ONLY so that another thread
+        // (or a signal handler) can wake a blocked event loop, and this port
+        // is built with QT_FEATURE_thread=OFF on a runtime that has neither
+        // threads nor signals -- nothing can ever call wakeUp() from outside
+        // the one thread that is already inside processEvents().
+        //
+        // pipe() genuinely fails here on a bare wasip2 host: wasi-libc routes
+        // it through its descriptor-table vtable and a host that does not
+        // implement it returns ENOTSUP ("QThreadPipe: Unable to create pipe:
+        // Not supported"). Upstream then qFatal()s in
+        // QEventDispatcherUNIXPrivate's constructor, so QApplication cannot be
+        // constructed AT ALL -- the whole of QtWidgets is unreachable because
+        // of a wakeup channel it never uses.
+        //
+        // fds stays {-1, -1}; prepare() hands poll() a negative fd, which
+        // POSIX requires it to ignore (revents is set to 0), and wakeUp() and
+        // check() below skip their I/O.
+        fds[0] = fds[1] = -1;
+#  else
         perror("QThreadPipe: Unable to create pipe");
         return false;
+#  endif
     }
 #endif
 
@@ -150,6 +178,15 @@ pollfd QThreadPipe::prepare() const
 
 void QThreadPipe::wakeUp()
 {
+#ifdef Q_OS_WASI
+    // No pipe (see init()). Still record the wakeup flag so that a
+    // wakeUp()-before-processEvents() is not lost; there is simply no fd to
+    // poke, because there is no other thread that could be blocked on it.
+    if (fds[1] < 0) {
+        wakeUps.fetchAndOrAcquire(1);
+        return;
+    }
+#endif
     if ((wakeUps.fetchAndOrAcquire(1) & 1) == 0) {
 #  ifdef EFD_CLOEXEC
         eventfd_write(fds[0], 1);
@@ -164,6 +201,14 @@ int QThreadPipe::check(const pollfd &pfd)
 {
     Q_ASSERT(pfd.fd == fds[0]);
 
+#ifdef Q_OS_WASI
+    // No pipe (see init()); poll() left revents at 0 for the negative fd, so
+    // there is nothing to drain. Report the pending wakeup, if any, so
+    // processEvents() still returns true for a wakeUp() that arrived earlier.
+    if (fds[0] < 0)
+        return wakeUps.testAndSetRelease(1, 0) ? 1 : 0;
+#endif
+
     char c[16];
     const int readyread = pfd.revents & POLLIN;
 
diff --git a/src/corelib/kernel/qcoreapplication.cpp b/src/corelib/kernel/qcoreapplication.cpp
index 8440235..86b0b68 100644
--- a/src/corelib/kernel/qcoreapplication.cpp
+++ b/src/corelib/kernel/qcoreapplication.cpp
@@ -465,7 +465,12 @@ QCoreApplicationPrivate::QCoreApplicationPrivate(int &aargc, char **aargv)
 #ifndef QT_NO_QOBJECT
     QCoreApplicationPrivate::is_app_closing = false;
 
-#  if defined(Q_OS_UNIX)
+#  if defined(Q_OS_UNIX) && !defined(Q_OS_WASI)
+    // Skipped on WASI: wasi-libc declares neither geteuid nor getuid (unistd.h
+    // keeps both behind __wasilibc_unmodified_upstream), so this is a compile
+    // error rather than a link error. There is also nothing to check -- a wasm
+    // component has no uid, cannot be setuid, and its authority comes from the
+    // capabilities the host handed it, not from a filesystem bit.
     if (Q_UNLIKELY(!setuidAllowed && (geteuid() != getuid())))
         qFatal("FATAL: The application binary appears to be running setuid, this is a security hole.");
 #  endif // Q_OS_UNIX
